Title: The ice that refused to melt
Credit: Written by
Author: Adil Chandio
Draft date: 2026-09-21
Contact: operator@monarch.local

/* M3_script fixture: 18 spoken beats x 7 words = the maths line for a 60s
   short at 3.5s clips. Visual direction travels inline as
   ::visual:: spoken line, so the visual never counts as spoken words.
   A character cue and its dialogue must be separated from action by a blank
   line (Fountain law), so every beat is written as its own dialogue block. */


EXT. GLACIER - DAY #1#

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::cracked glacier face:: The ice refused to melt for years

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::calendar pages flipping:: Then one ordinary summer it simply vanished

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::drone shot over a dark lake:: Scientists measured the dark lake beneath it


EXT. LAKE - CONTINUOUS #2#

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::thermometer glowing red:: Nobody expected the water to be warm

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::steam curling off black water:: Warm water means heat and hidden pressure

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::stickman pointing down at the cliff:: That heat came from the ground itself


INT. HEAT CUTAWAY - NIGHT #3#

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::volcano cutaway under a glacier:: Old magma chambers still breathe under glaciers

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::bubbles climbing through meltwater:: Meltwater sank and touched that hot rock

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::diagram loop with rising arrows:: It rose like a slow boiling pot


EXT. ICE SHELF - LATER #4#

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::ice column thinning from below:: Warm water eats the ice from below

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::shelf cracking and calving off:: The shelf lost its grip and fell

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::lake draining into a hole:: The lake drained under the ice sheet


INT. OBSERVATORY - NIGHT #5#

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::satellite passing over white ice:: Satellites watched the whole white surface drop

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::seismograph needle jumping hard:: Seismic sensors heard the ice crack apart

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::scientist staring at printed numbers:: The numbers were not in any model


EXT. KITCHEN - DAWN #6#

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::model diagram with a wrong arrow:: The models assumed cold ice stays cold

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::map slowly turning red:: One warm year rewrote the whole map

VOICEOVER (V.O.)
::stickman holding a glass of ice water:: Your drink is melting the same way
